<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mary Shelley</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1797">1797</a>–<a href="/years/1851">1851</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Mary Wollstonecraft Shelley</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mary Shelley was an English novelist who wrote the Gothic novel <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankenstein">Frankenstein; or, The Modern Prometheus</a>, considered an early example of science fiction. The daughter of William Godwin and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mary-wollstonecraft">Mary Wollstonecraft</a>, she eloped with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/percy-bysshe-shelley">Percy Bysshe Shelley</a> in 1814 and married him in 1816. The novel grew from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lord-byron">Lord Byron</a>'s proposal, during a wet summer beside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-geneva">Lake Geneva</a> in 1816, that they each write a ghost story, and was published anonymously in 1818. Widowed in 1822, she became a professional author, writing novels such as The Last Man and editing her husband's poems.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mary-shelley.jpg" alt="Mary Shelley" width="320" height="391" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Richard Rothwell · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Richard_Rothwell_(1800-1868)_-_Mary_Wollstonecraft_Shelley_-_NPG_1235_-_National_Portrait_Gallery.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Mary Wollstonecraft Godwin was born in Somers Town, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, on 30 August 1797, the child of the philosopher William Godwin and the women's rights advocate Mary Wollstonecraft, who died of puerperal fever eleven days after the birth. Godwin brought her up with her older half-sister, Fanny Imlay, gave her a rich informal education and encouraged her in his own anarchist political theories. When she was four, he married a neighbour, Mary Jane Clairmont, with whom Mary had a troubled relationship.</p><p>In 1814 she began a romance with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/percy-bysshe-shelley">Percy Bysshe Shelley</a>, a follower of her father who was already married. With her stepsister Claire Clairmont they left for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and travelled through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. After their return they faced ostracism, constant debt and the death of their daughter. Percy's wife Harriet was found drowned in December 1816, and Mary and Percy married on 30 December.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In May 1816 the couple and Claire Clairmont went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geneva">Geneva</a> to spend the summer with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lord-byron">Lord Byron</a>, who rented the Villa Diodati with his physician John William Polidori. There Mary conceived the idea for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankenstein">Frankenstein; or, The Modern Prometheus</a>, published in 1818. How far Percy contributed to it is argued: James Rieger saw him as editor or minor collaborator, while Anne K. Mellor held that he only made technical corrections.</p><p>The Shelleys left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> in 1818. Two of their children died there, and Percy Florence was born on 12 November 1819. In 1822 Percy drowned when his boat sank in a storm near Viareggio, and a year later she returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> as a professional author. Her novels include Valperga, The Last Man (1826), Lodore and Falkner, and she wrote biographies for Dionysius Lardner's Cabinet Cyclopaedia between 1832 and 1839. She published Percy's Posthumous Poems in 1824 and a new edition of his poetry in 1839.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>The last decade of her life was dogged by illness, most likely caused by the brain tumour that killed her at the age of 53, on 1 February 1851. Until the 1970s she was known mainly for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankenstein">Frankenstein</a> and her efforts to publish her husband's works. Recent scholarship finds in her other writing a political radical who argued that cooperation and sympathy, as practised by women in the family, were the ways to reform society.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Mary Shelley” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The cold, wet summer of 1816, which followed the eruption of Mount Tambora in Indonesia, kept Lord Byron and his guests indoors at the Villa Diodati by Lake Geneva, where his ghost-story contest led <b>Mary Shelley</b> to write Frankenstein.</blockquote><a href="/movement/romanticism">Romanticism · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Mary Shelley</b>'s tale of a scientist and his creation is often called the first science-fiction novel.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/january-1">1 January in history</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Somers Town, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=51.53,-0.13&amp;year=1832" aria-label="Somers Town on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Mary Shelley"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4993 5722h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4993 5722h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=51.53,-0.13&amp;year=1832">What was here in 1832? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-family"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Family</h2><ul class="enc-seealso enc-fam"><li><a href="/encyclopedia/mary-wollstonecraft"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/mary-wollstonecraft.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Mary Wollstonecraft</span><span class="tp">Mother &#183; 1759–1797</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/percy-bysshe-shelley"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/percy-bysshe-shelley.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Percy Bysshe Shelley</span><span class="tp">Married &#183; 1792–1822</span></span></a></li></ul><p class="ft-note">Parents, spouses and children as Wikidata records them, among the people of the encyclopedia: not the whole family.</p></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Everywhere I see bliss, from which I alone am irrevocably excluded. I was benevolent and good; misery made me a fiend.&#8221;</p><footer>Frankenstein (1818), ch. 10 &#183; original &#183; spoken by the creature to his maker &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/84/pg84-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;…how dangerous is the acquirement of knowledge and how much happier that man is who believes his native town to be the world, than he who aspires to become greater than his nature will allow.&#8221;</p><footer>Frankenstein (1818), ch. 4 &#183; original &#183; spoken by Victor Frankenstein &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/84/pg84-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Nothing is so painful to the human mind as a great and sudden change.&#8221;</p><footer>Frankenstein (1818), ch. 9 &#183; original &#183; spoken by Victor Frankenstein &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/84/pg84-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><details class="enc-q-more"><summary>2 more</summary><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Remember that I am thy creature; I ought to be thy Adam, but I am rather the fallen angel, whom thou drivest from joy for no misdeed.&#8221;</p><footer>Frankenstein (1818), ch. 10 &#183; original &#183; spoken by the creature to his maker &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/84/pg84-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Life, although it may only be an accumulation of anguish, is dear to me, and I will defend it.&#8221;</p><footer>Frankenstein (1818), ch. 10 &#183; original &#183; spoken by the creature to his maker &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/84/pg84-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote></details><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 595 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
